  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 6,
    "lesson_title": "Determining Stability from RLC Poles",
    "lesson_core_move": "Determine whether an RLC circuit transfer function is BIBO stable by checking that all computed poles lie strictly in the left half of the s-plane and interpreting any right-half-plane or imaginary-axis pole as a nondecaying circuit mode.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Determining Stability from RLC Poles",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Determining Stability from RLC Poles",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For an RLC transfer function, the stability cue is the denominator of $H(s)$. Write the transfer function in the rational form $H(s)=N(s)/D(s)$, then treat the poles as the roots of $D(s)=0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "H(s)=\\frac{N(s)}{D(s)}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The test is a left-half-plane test. The circuit is BIBO stable only when every pole $s_k$ has negative real part, written as $\\Re\\{s_k\\}<0$. A right-half-plane pole grows, and an imaginary-axis pole does not decay, so either case fails the BIBO stability test.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\Re\\{s_k\\}<0 \\text{ for all poles } s_k \\Rightarrow \\text{ stable; } \\exists\\,s_k:\\Re\\{s_k\\}\\ge 0 \\Rightarrow \\text{ unstable}.",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In the simplest base case $H(s)=1/(s+3)$, the denominator is $D(s)=s+3$. Solving $s+3=0$ gives the single pole $s=-3$, and $\\Re\\{-3\\}=-3<0$, so this transfer function is stable.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For higher-order RLC denominators, repeat the same check for each root of $D(s)$. A single pole with $\\Re\\{s_k\\}>0$ or $\\Re\\{s_k\\}=0$ determines the classification, because one growing or nondecaying circuit mode is enough to prevent BIBO stability.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Determining Stability from RLC Poles",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Classifying Stability from Negative Real RLC Poles",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For $H(s)=\\frac{6}{(s+4)(s+1)}$, classify the system as BIBO stable, unstable, or nondecaying.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start with the denominator, because BIBO stability for this RLC transfer function is decided by the pole locations. The denominator is already factored, so set $D(s)=0$ and use each factor to find a pole.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "D(s)=(s+4)(s+1)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "D(s)=0 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad (s+4)(s+1)=0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Solving each factor gives the two real poles $s=-4$ and $s=-1$. Both have strictly negative real part, so both poles lie in the left half of the $s$-plane.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "s=-4 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad s=-1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because every pole satisfies $\\operatorname{Re}(s)<0$, the modes decay instead of growing or persisting forever. The transfer function is therefore BIBO stable.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Determining Stability from RLC Poles",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Classifying Stability from Left-Half-Plane Complex RLC Poles",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $H(s)=\\frac{40}{s^2+2s+10}$, find the poles and decide if the RLC system is stable.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that stability is decided by the pole locations of $H(s)$. The poles come from the denominator, so set the characteristic polynomial equal to zero.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "s^2+2s+10=0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the quadratic formula. The negative discriminant means the poles form a complex-conjugate pair.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned} s &= \\frac{-2\\pm\\sqrt{2^2-4(1)(10)}}{2} \\\\ &= \\frac{-2\\pm\\sqrt{-36}}{2} \\\\ &= -1\\pm 3j \\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The poles are $s=-1+3j$ and $s=-1-3j$. Both have real part $-1$, so both poles lie strictly in the left half-plane.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\operatorname{Re}(-1+3j)=\\operatorname{Re}(-1-3j)=-1<0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Therefore the RLC transfer function is BIBO stable. The complex pair means the natural response oscillates, but the negative real part makes that oscillation decay; there are no right-half-plane or imaginary-axis poles to create a nondecaying mode.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Determining Stability from RLC Poles",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Classifying Instability from a Right-Half-Plane RLC Pole",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Classify $H(s)=\\frac{5}{(s-2)(s+1)(s+4)}$: is this RLC transfer function BIBO stable or unstable?",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The stability cue is the pole locations, not the numerator value. For a rational RLC transfer function, BIBO stability requires every pole to satisfy $\\Re\\{s\\}<0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "H(s)=\\frac{5}{(s-2)(s+1)(s+4)}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Set each denominator factor equal to zero to list the poles.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "s-2=0\\Rightarrow s=2,\\qquad s+1=0\\Rightarrow s=-1,\\qquad s+4=0\\Rightarrow s=-4",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The poles $s=-1$ and $s=-4$ are in the left half-plane, but $s=2$ has positive real part because $\\Re\\{2\\}=2>0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\Re\\{2\\}=2>0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Therefore the transfer function is BIBO unstable. One right-half-plane pole is enough to fail the all-poles test, even though the other two poles are stable left-half-plane poles.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Determining Stability from RLC Poles",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Classifying Instability from Imaginary-Axis RLC Poles",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Classify stability for $H(s)=\\frac{2}{s^2+9}$ and interpret the time-domain implication of poles on the imaginary axis.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the denominator $s^2+9$. For this lesson's BIBO rule, the transfer function is stable only when every pole has strictly negative real part.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "s^2+9=0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "s^2=-9 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad s=\\pm 3j",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Both poles are purely imaginary. Their real parts are exactly zero, so they sit on the boundary of the left half-plane rather than strictly inside it.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\Re\\{3j\\}=0,\\qquad \\Re\\{-3j\\}=0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because the poles do not satisfy $\\Re\\{s\\}<0$, the circuit transfer function is classified as unstable for BIBO purposes in this topic context.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The time-domain meaning is a nondecaying sinusoidal mode. These imaginary-axis poles do not create exponential growth like a right-half-plane pole, but they also do not decay away like left-half-plane poles.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
